---
import { link } from "../../../lib/url";
import fs from "node:fs";
import path from "node:path";
import Base from "../../../layouts/Base.astro";
import { GENERATED, SOURCES, CPI_FIRST, CPI_LAST, monthLabel, PLFS_YEARS, HCES_YEARS } from "../../../lib/jobs-prices/data";

const report = fs.readFileSync(path.resolve(process.cwd(), "..", "jobs-prices", "data", "validation_report.md"), "utf8");
const m = report.match(/\*\*Result: (\w+)\*\* \(([\d,]+) checks, ([\d,]+) failures\)/);
const sections = [...report.matchAll(/^## (✅|❌) (.+)$/gm)].map((x) => ({ ok: x[1] === "✅", title: x[2] }));
const checks = m ? Number(m[2]).toLocaleString("en-IN") : "?";
/** How many cases the report lists under the reason that starts with `start`. */
const count = (start: string) => {
  const hit = [...report.matchAll(/Allowed \((\d+)\): (.{0,80})/g)].filter((x) => x[2].startsWith(start));
  return hit.reduce((n, x) => n + Number(x[1]), 0).toLocaleString("en-IN");
};
const files = [
  ["jobs_prices.json", "What the site shows, in compact form"],
  ["plfs_rates.csv", "Participation, worker ratio and unemployment: every published combination, with the reason where one is left out"],
  ["plfs_workers.csv", "Workers by type of work, industry, employer and occupation"],
  ["plfs_conditions.csv", "Regular employees without a written contract, paid leave or social security"],
  ["plfs_earnings.csv", "Average earnings from regular, casual and self-employed work"],
  ["cpi.csv", "Consumer Price Index (base 2012): all groups for All India, the general index for every state"],
  ["cpialrl.csv", "Consumer Price Index for agricultural and rural labourers"],
  ["hces.csv", "Household consumption spending"],
  ["sources.csv", "Every downloaded file: address, fingerprint (SHA-256) and date"],
  ["validation_report.md", "Every check and every documented exception"],
];
const updated = new Date(GENERATED).toLocaleDateString("en-IN", { day: "numeric", month: "long", year: "numeric" });
const nFiles = Object.keys(SOURCES).length;
---
<Base title="Sources and checks" section="about" description="Where every jobs, prices and spending figure on India Data Review comes from, how it is checked, and the errors found in the official data.">
  <h1>Sources and checks</h1>
  <p>
    All figures in this section come from the Ministry of Statistics and Programme Implementation (MoSPI), through its
    official data service (<a href="https://esankhyiki.mospi.gov.in" rel="external noopener">eSankhyiki</a>, api.mospi.gov.in).
    A program downloads {nFiles} files, keeps a copy of each with its fingerprint (SHA-256), builds the data and checks
    it. Data last built {updated}.
  </p>

  <section class="card">
    <h2>What is covered</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Jobs</strong>: Periodic Labour Force Survey (PLFS). Annual figures for calendar years {PLFS_YEARS[0]} to {PLFS_YEARS[PLFS_YEARS.length - 1]} (MoSPI's data service does not have the earlier July–June annual figures, except job conditions from 2017-18); quarterly figures for towns and cities from April–June 2018, and for villages too from 2025; monthly figures from January 2025.</li>
      <li><strong>Consumer prices</strong>: Consumer Price Index, base 2012, {monthLabel(CPI_FIRST)} to {monthLabel(CPI_LAST)}: every group and item group for All India, the general index for every state. The new base-2024 series (from January 2026) is not yet in MoSPI's data service.</li>
      <li><strong>Prices for farm and rural labourers</strong>: the Labour Bureau's CPI-AL and CPI-RL, base 1986-87 and, from June 2025, base 2019.</li>
      <li><strong>Household spending</strong>: Household Consumption Expenditure Survey, {HCES_YEARS.join(" and ")}.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="card">
    <h2>Checked automatically</h2>
    <p>
      <strong>{checks} checks{m && m[1] === "PASSED" ? ", all passing" : ""}.</strong> The site cannot be built unless
      every check passes. Known problems in the official data are allowed only with a written reason, listed in the report.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Jobs</strong>: the three rates are tied together (worker ratio = participation × (1 − unemployment rate)), and every published combination is tested; the figure for everyone must lie between women's and men's, and the rural + urban figure between rural and urban; shares of workers by industry, type of work or employer must add up to 100%.</li>
      <li><strong>Prices</strong>: every published inflation rate is worked out again from the index a year earlier; the combined index must lie between the rural and urban index; no month may be missing.</li>
      <li><strong>Spending</strong>: the twelve spending classes, weighted by the share of people in each, must give the average; item categories must add up to the food and non-food totals, and those to the average.</li>
    </ul>
    <ul class="checklist small">
      {sections.map((s) => <li>{s.ok ? "✅" : "❌"} {s.title}</li>)}
    </ul>
    <p class="small"><a href={link("/data/jobs-prices/validation_report.md")}>Full validation report</a></p>
  </section>

  <section class="card">
    <h2>Problems found in the official data</h2>
    <p class="small muted">MoSPI's data service appears to have been filled partly by reading printed tables, and the checks find the slips. None of these figures is shown; every one is listed in the report and kept, with its reason, in the downloads.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Jobs figures that cannot all be right: {count("MoSPI's PLFS figures")} combinations.</strong> Letters for digits ("l0.8", "IN.4", "20J") and figures that break the rules above. Most are in the quarterly series; April–June 2019, for example, shows urban unemployment of 21.6% where MoSPI's bulletin for that quarter says 8.9%. Each such combination is left out whole, since which of its numbers is wrong cannot be told.</li>
      <li><strong>All-India shares of workers by type of work</strong> (self-employed, regular, casual) do not add up to 100% (for example 0.3%, 0.4%, 0.6% and 0.6% for women in 2022), while every state's do. The site shows this breakdown by state only.</li>
      <li><strong>{count("MoSPI's data service returns some")} rows of "distribution of workers" with no breakdown named</strong>, often with several different values for the same cell. They cannot be read.</li>
      <li>For 2025 in current weekly status, the "all self-employed" share is not the sum of its parts; the site uses usual-status figures.</li>
      <li><strong>Household spending by item category</strong> filed under six states (rural, 2022-23) does not add up to those states' own average and appears to be other states' figures (Goa's adds up to Andhra Pradesh's average, Haryana's to Assam's). Those breakdowns are left out.</li>
      <li>The farm labourers' index for Delhi (new series) is published as 0.00; it is treated as not compiled.</li>
      <li>{count("MoSPI's data service returns characters")} other figures are published as stray characters (such as a backtick) instead of numbers.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="card">
    <h2>How the figures are used</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Annual job figures</strong> use usual status (what a person mainly did over the past year) for people aged 15 and over; quarterly and monthly figures use current weekly status (the past seven days). The two are not mixed in one chart.</li>
      <li><strong>Yearly inflation</strong> is the change in the average of the twelve monthly indices, worked out here from MoSPI's indices, as the RBI and MoSPI state yearly averages.</li>
      <li><strong>Earnings after inflation</strong> divide average earnings by the average consumer price index of the same calendar year (rural, urban or combined, to match).</li>
      <li><strong>Household spending</strong> excludes items received free unless a chart says otherwise.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="card">
    <h2>Not here yet</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>The new Consumer Price Index on base 2024, and the new bases of industrial production and wholesale prices, when MoSPI's data service carries them.</li>
      <li>Annual PLFS figures for the July–June years 2017-18 to 2023-24, from MoSPI's annual reports.</li>
      <li>State comparisons from the RBI's Handbook of Statistics on Indian States (income per person, wage rates).</li>
      <li>The Time Use Survey and the Multiple Indicator Survey (MoSPI's data service did not return them when tried).</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="card">
    <h2>Download the data</h2>
    <ul>
      {files.map(([f, d]) => <li><a href={link(`/data/jobs-prices/${f}`)}>{f}</a>: {d}</li>)}
    </ul>
  </section>
</Base>

<style>
  .checklist { list-style: none; padding: 0; }
  .checklist li { padding: 2px 0; }
</style>
